# {{ project_name }}

{{ description }}

A quant research project in one place: study a signal (IC and rank IC with Newey-West
t-statistics, quantile spreads, IC decay up to {{ max_horizon }} bars), backtest it as
dollar-neutral weights (lagged trades, monthly rebalancing with drift, {{ cost_bps }} bps costs,
delistings), and report the results with computed figures apart from interpretation. The study
and the backtest share data, signals, metrics, and one trade-timing convention.
Generated by Quant App Factory from the `quant-baseline` template pack. The code is yours.

## Use

```bash
uv sync
uv run {{ project_name }} study --demo       # a random walk: expect no edge (a sanity check)
uv run {{ project_name }} study             # IC, spreads, decay (data from config.toml)
uv run {{ project_name }} backtest          # net of costs
uv run {{ project_name }} walk-forward      # out of sample only
uv run {{ project_name }} report                                    # the latest run
```

Set `data` in `config.toml` once ({% if 'data-database' in components %}it is `"database"` already; {% endif %}a price file, or `"demo"`), and
`study`, `backtest`, and `walk-forward` need no data flag; `--prices`, `--demo`{% if 'data-database' in components %},
`--database`{% endif %} override it for one run.

Parameters live in `config.toml`: the signal (12-1 momentum or one-month reversal by
default), the trade lag, quantiles, costs, the rebalance schedule, and the delisting return.
Flags such as `--signal reversal --lookback 5` override them for one run.

Each run gets its own folder, `results/<UTC time>-<kind>-<signal>/` (or `--label NAME`), and
never overwrites another. It holds `results.json`, with the metrics, series, parameters, a
SHA-256 fingerprint of the price data, the code version (`git describe` in a git checkout),
and the time, plus `config.toml`, the exact parameters: `--config results/<run>/config.toml`
repeats the run. `report RUN_FOLDER` renders a specific run, with a calendar-year table and an
equity curve (`report-equity.svg`). `report RUN_A RUN_B ...` compares runs side by side, with
parameters, metrics, calendar years, provenance, and one chart, in `results/comparison.md`.

`exposure` chooses the book. `dollar-neutral` (the default) ranks the signal into zero net
dollars. `beta-neutral` also removes beta to the equal-weight market, using rolling betas from
past data only. `long-only` holds the top signal quantile equally and reports information
ratio, tracking error, and active return against the equal-weight market. Every backtest
reports its realised beta.

`walk-forward` is the honest number. It rolls a training window (`train_bars`) and a test
window (`test_bars`) through the data. In each fold it picks the lookback with the best training
score (net Sharpe, or rank IC with `objective = "ic"`) from a small grid (`lookbacks`, by default
0.5, 0.75, and 1 times the standard). It sees only prices up to the end of the training window,
and it reports the test windows only.

Price files are wide: a `date` column, then one column of prices per asset. Dates are ISO
(`2024-01-09`), unique, and ascending{% if 'data-parquet' in components %}; `.parquet` files work
too, with text or date columns{% endif %}.
